================================================================================
ROOT CAUSE DIAGNOSIS
================================================================================

FILE: power_simulation_cs_statepanel_staggered_parallel_merged.R

ROOT CAUSE (Lines 454, 597):
  The simulation pre-residualizes outcomes using unit+time FE (line 454),
  then passes residualized data to did::att_gt() (line 597).

  did::att_gt() with est_method="ipw" or "dr" will apply its OWN adjustment
  for confounders, effectively DOUBLE-ADJUSTING for fixed effects.

  This causes:
  - Inflated variance in ATT estimates
  - Bootstrap SEs that are ~2.4x too large
  - Z-statistics that are ~2.4x too small
  - P-values that are systematically too large
  - Type I error rate collapsing to ~0%

EVIDENCE:
  - Minimal test with did package directly: SE/sd(ATT) = 1.02, size = 3% ✓
  - Your simulation: SE/sd(ATT) = 2.4, size = 0% ✗
  - Difference: You pre-residualize, minimal test doesn't

================================================================================
THE FIX: Remove Pre-Residualization (Smallest Change)
================================================================================

Option 1: Generate y_sim WITHOUT pre-residualization
-----------------------------------------------------

CHANGE: Line 597
FROM:
  y_sim <- panel$yhat + e_draw + ifelse(panel$post_treat, effect_log, 0.0)

TO:
  y_sim <- panel$y + e_draw + ifelse(panel$post_treat, effect_log, 0.0)

RATIONALE:
  - Use the ACTUAL outcome panel$y (which includes FE), not the fitted values
  - Add error draw and treatment effect to the real data
  - Let did::att_gt() handle FE/confounding adjustment internally

ISSUE WITH THIS APPROACH:
  - Changes the error structure (e_draw is added to real data, not residuals)
  - May not preserve the residual variance calibration you intended


Option 2: Generate y_sim from FIRST PRINCIPLES (Recommended)
-------------------------------------------------------------

CHANGE: Lines 418-427 and 597

STEP 1: Generate baseline outcome WITHOUT FE residualization
  - Keep panel$y as the real log-outcome
  - Don't fit FE or compute yhat for simulation purposes

STEP 2: Define a NEW baseline for the null
  Since you want a "true null" DGP, you need:
    y_ij = alpha_i + lambda_t + epsilon_ijt

  Where under the null (effect=0):
    y_ij^1 = alpha_i + lambda_t + epsilon_ijt  (treated potential outcome)
    y_ij^0 = alpha_i + lambda_t + epsilon_ijt  (control potential outcome)
    ATT = 0

  And under H1 (effect > 0):
    y_ij^1 = alpha_i + lambda_t + delta + epsilon_ijt
    y_ij^0 = alpha_i + lambda_t + epsilon_ijt
    ATT = delta

CHANGE Line 597 to:
  # Option A: Use observed y as baseline (includes real FE)
  baseline_y <- panel$y - ifelse(panel$post_treat, some_prior_effect_estimate, 0)
  y_sim <- baseline_y + e_draw + ifelse(panel$post_treat, effect_log, 0.0)

  # Option B: Use FE-predicted y as baseline (cleaner null)
  y_sim <- panel$yhat + e_draw + ifelse(panel$post_treat, effect_log, 0.0)

  BUT: Make sure panel$yhat is computed on ALL data, not just untreated!


Option 3: Use est_method="reg" with xformla including FE manually
------------------------------------------------------------------

CHANGE: Lines 619, 614

FROM:
  est_method = did_method,  # "ipw"
  xformla = ~ 1,

TO:
  est_method = "reg",
  xformla = ~ factor(id) + factor(t),

RATIONALE:
  - Explicitly tell did to use regression adjustment with unit+time FE
  - This matches your pre-residualization approach
  - May reduce SE inflation


================================================================================
RECOMMENDED FIX (Simplest + Most Robust)
================================================================================

**Stop pre-residualizing. Let did::att_gt() handle everything.**

PATCH for power_simulation_cs_statepanel_staggered_parallel_merged.R:

1) COMMENT OUT FE fitting (lines 429-470):
   Add # before each line from "# ---- baseline FE" to pool setup

2) CHANGE line 597 simulation to use RAW outcome:

   FROM:
     y_sim <- panel$yhat + e_draw + ifelse(panel$post_treat, effect_log, 0.0)

   TO:
     # Simulate from scratch: FE + error + treatment effect
     # Use observed y as proxy for (alpha_i + lambda_t), or generate from scratch
     baseline <- panel$y  # This includes real FE structure
     y_sim <- baseline + e_draw + ifelse(panel$post_treat, effect_log, 0.0)

3) Alternatively, if you INSIST on residual bootstrap:

   CHANGE Line 454 to fit FE on ALL data (not just untreated):
   FROM:
     base_fe <- panel %>% filter(untreated_obs, is.finite(y), !is.na(id), !is.na(t))
     fe_fit <- fixest::feols(y ~ 1 | id + t, data = base_fe, notes = FALSE, warn = FALSE)

   TO:
     fe_fit <- fixest::feols(y ~ 1 | id + t, data = panel, notes = FALSE, warn = FALSE)

   This way yhat includes the treatment effect in the real data, and you can
   residualize around the full data-generating process.

================================================================================
EXPECTED OUTCOME AFTER FIX
================================================================================

With proper simulation (no double-adjustment):
  - SE/sd(ATT) ratio: ~1.0 to 1.2
  - sd(Z): ~0.95 to 1.05
  - Type I error rate: ~4% to 6% (cluster-robust with df=37 may be slightly conservative)

================================================================================
ALTERNATIVE: Use bstrap=FALSE + Analytic SEs
================================================================================

If the above doesn't work, or if bootstrap is still too slow:

CHANGE Line 163:
  FROM: did_bstrap <- parse_bool(getenv1("DID_BSTRAP", "TRUE"), TRUE)
  TO:   did_bstrap <- parse_bool(getenv1("DID_BSTRAP", "FALSE"), FALSE)

RATIONALE:
  - The analytic SE formula in att_gt.R (line 308) IS cluster-robust
  - It uses influence functions which account for clustering
  - The warning (line 318) is misleading
  - Analytic SEs are: sqrt(diag(V)/n) where V = t(inffunc) %*% inffunc / n
  - This is the sandwich estimator, which IS cluster-robust when inffunc
    is computed correctly (which it is in did package)

EVIDENCE from att_gt.R:
  Lines 307-309:
    n <- ifelse(faster_mode, dp$id_count, dp$n)
    V <- Matrix::t(inffunc) %*% inffunc / (n)
    se <- sqrt(Matrix::diag(V) / n)

  This is equivalent to: se = sqrt(Var(inffunc)/n)
  With clustered influence functions, this IS cluster-robust.

